Typecheck: `target[property]` where property: string | symbol — target is Record<string, unknown> → symbol not usable as index. Fix: constrain handler property to `string`:

```ts
get(target: Record<string, unknown>, property: string) {
  schemaReads += 1;
  return target[property];
}
```

Proxy handler's get can declare a narrower param type than string|symbol? The handler type is ProxyHandler<T> with get?(target: T, p: string | symbol, receiver: unknown): any — a function accepting fewer/narrower params is assignable (parameter bivariance for method-style... for function properties, strictFunctionTypes: parameter contravariance — handler get param `string` is NOT assignable where `string | symbol` required under strictFunctionTypes!). Hmm — strict:true → strictFunctionTypes on → ProxyHandler<T>['get'] expects (target, p: string|symbol, receiver). Providing (target: Record<string,unknown>, property: string) — param contravariance: required param type string|symbol must be assignable TO my param type string — string|symbol is NOT assignable to string → error under strictFunctionTypes.

But wait — ProxyHandler.get's TARGET type: `new Proxy<T extends object>(target: T, handler: ProxyHandler<T>)`. My T = the schema literal type. If I make the target `Record<string, unknown>` TOO: `new Proxy({...} as Record<string, unknown>, {get(target: Record<string, unknown>, property: string | symbol)})` — target[property] still symbol-index error.

Cleaner: 
```ts
get(target, property) {
  schemaReads += 1;
  return Reflect.get(target, property);
}
```
Reflect.get(target, property: string|symbol) ✓ typed for any object. And target inferred from T. So:

```ts
const schema = new Proxy(
  {type: 'object', properties: {query: {type: 'string'}}, required: ['query']},
  {
    get(target, property) {
      schemaReads += 1;
      return Reflect.get(target, property);
    },
  }
);
```
Hmm — unannotated params in contextually-typed position ✓ inferred. Reflect.get(target, property) — target: the literal type, property: string|symbol → Reflect.get(obj: object, key: string|symbol) ✓.

Hmm wait, but does `Reflect.get` count toward no-duplicate-code same as before? Same tokens roughly. And "return target[property]" → "return Reflect.get(target, property);" — one more token, fine.

Hmm — but `declare-near-use`, fine. Also eslint no-undef... Reflect is ES2015 global ✓.

Wait, actually simpler alternative: keep `target[property as string]`?? — property could be symbol → `'' + property`... no. Reflect.get is the clean fix.

Also — will the Proxy WITHOUT type annotation on get still make `schema` assignable to inputSchema (Record<string, unknown>)? Proxy<T> = T ✓.

Apply to both Proxy blocks.

